{
  "opportunity_id": "proc_7b2079ed91",
  "source": "content_cluster",
  "title": "Sitara deal price corrections chased by hand before invoicing",
  "task_class": "deal_entry_correction",
  "label_mechanical": "Deal entry and correction in Sitara / Unify / TAGG: correct deal / system / trading",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "Someone comparing a counterparty invoice, a confirmation or a settlement statement against the deal ticket in Sitara finds the price or volume on the ticket does not match what was actually agreed or paid. They email the deal owner or a support desk with the deal ticket number, state what Sitara currently shows and what it should show, and ask for the ticket to be changed. The requester then waits for the change to be made and confirmed before they can invoice, path the volume in balance, or settle. Some threads close with a short confirmation that the system now agrees; others get escalated when the requester cannot edit the ticket themselves, as when access to an older deal is refused.",
    "where_it_stalls": "The person who spots the discrepancy is usually not the person who can fix it. Requests are sent as free-text mail naming a ticket number and a corrected figure, then sit until the owner acts \u2014 the rework signal is present on nearly every message, and several are escalations rather than first asks. One thread shows a requester locked out of the Sitara ticket entirely because of the deal's origin, so the correction can only proceed through someone else. Meanwhile downstream work is blocked: an invoice going out the next day, or a path that will not balance until the volume is nudged.",
    "why_it_recurs": "Deal tickets are captured once and then checked against counterparty invoices and confirmations later, so mismatches surface only at settlement or invoicing time, when a monthly billing cycle forces the comparison.",
    "automation_opportunity": "A recurring exception report that matches Sitara ticket prices and volumes against counterparty invoice and confirmation data and lists only the mismatches would surface these before invoicing rather than after. Pairing it with a structured correction request \u2014 ticket number, current value, requested value, requester, owner \u2014 routed to whoever holds edit rights would remove the free-text chasing and make the open items visible.",
    "coherence": "tight"
  },
